sections.body:
You'll often want a prop to have a fallback when the parent doesn't pass it. Since props arrive as one object that you destructure in the parameter list, ordinary JavaScript default-value syntax works:

```jsx
function Button({ label, variant = "primary" }) {
  return <button className={variant}>{label}</button>;
}

<Button label="Save" />                 // variant defaults to "primary"
<Button label="Delete" variant="danger" /> // variant is "danger"
```

A default only applies when the prop is `undefined`: the parent didn't pass it, or passed `variant={undefined}`. It does **not** apply to other falsy values. `<Button label="Save" variant={null} />` or `<Button label="Save" variant={0} />` will NOT fall back to `"primary"`; the component receives `null` or `0`, because JavaScript's default values check for `undefined` and nothing broader. People often expect "falsy means use the default", and that expectation produces bugs. Also note that the older `Component.defaultProps` static is no longer supported on function components in React 19; default values in the destructuring list are the way to do it.

`children` is not special React syntax. It's an ordinary prop that React fills in with whatever you nest between a component's opening and closing JSX tags:

```jsx
function Card({ title, children }) {
  return (
    <div className="card">
      <h3>{title}</h3>
      <div className="card-body">{children}</div>
    </div>
  );
}

<Card title="Profile">
  <p>Name: Amara</p>
  <button>Edit</button>
</Card>
```

Everything between `<Card ...>` and `</Card>` (the paragraph and the button) becomes `props.children` inside `Card`, and `Card` doesn't need to know what it is. That's what makes **composition** work: `Card` decides where the content goes (under a title, inside a bordered box), and the caller decides what the content is, without `Card` needing a separate prop for everything someone might put inside it. Layout components, modals and wrappers of every kind are built this way: accept `children`, decide how to arrange it, and let the call site supply it. A related convenience is spreading props through to a child (`<button {...rest}>`), which forwards everything you didn't destructure; in React 19 that includes `ref`, which function components now receive as an ordinary prop, so `forwardRef` is no longer needed for new code.

Data flows down, so a child can't reach up and change something the parent owns. How does a child report that something happened (a button was clicked, a value was typed)? It calls a **function the parent handed it as a prop**. This is the standard way a child communicates upward in React (context and refs are other channels, but this is the one you'll use most):

```jsx
function LikeButton({ isLiked, onToggle }) {
  return (
    <button onClick={onToggle}>
      {isLiked ? "\u2665 Liked" : "\u2661 Like"}
    </button>
  );
}

function Post({ post, onLikeToggle }) {
  return (
    <article>
      <p>{post.text}</p>
      <LikeButton isLiked={post.liked} onToggle={() => onLikeToggle(post.id)} />
    </article>
  );
}
```

`LikeButton` never touches `post` or any state; it calls `onToggle` when clicked. The parent (`Post`, or further up, whoever owns the `liked` state) decides what `onLikeToggle` does, typically calling a state setter. A useful naming convention: `onX` for a callback prop (`onToggle`, `onLikeToggle`, `onClick`) and `handleX` for the function that implements the response (`handleLikeToggle`). It makes the direction obvious: `onX` is what a child is given to call; `handleX` is the parent's implementation of what happens when it's called.

