sections.body:
Props and state are both data a component has access to while rendering, but they differ in **who owns them and who's allowed to change them.** A prop is external input, owned by the parent; the component only reads it (as the previous concept covered). **State** is data the component owns itself and changes over its lifetime in response to things like user interaction. Changing it is what makes the component re-render with new output.

In a function component, state is introduced with the `useState` hook (covered in full depth later in this course; here you only need the basic shape):

```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Clicked {count} times</button>;
}
```

`useState(0)` returns a pair: the current value (`count`) and a function to update it (`setCount`). Calling `setCount(...)` stores the new value for this component instance and tells React to re-render the component so its output reflects it. Props don't work this way: a prop changes only because the *parent* re-rendered and passed a new value down, and the child has no setter for its own props.

Objects and arrays make this harder to spot, because mutating one does change the underlying data. React just never finds out:

```jsx
function ProfileForm() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: "Amara", age: 29 });

  function haveBirthday() {
    user.age = user.age + 1; // mutates the object in place -- doesn't work
    console.log(user.age);   // logs the new age just fine!
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <p>{user.name} is {user.age}</p>
      <button onClick={haveBirthday}>Happy birthday</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

`user.age = user.age + 1` does mutate the object React is holding in state, and `console.log(user.age)` shows the new value. But React doesn't inspect the value it stores to detect internal changes; it only reacts to `setUser` being called. So the component never re-renders, and the paragraph keeps showing the old age even though the object has changed. The fix is to leave the existing object alone and give `setUser` a new one: `setUser({ ...user, age: user.age + 1 })`. That updates the stored value and, being a call to the setter, triggers a re-render.

examples.code:
function BrokenCounter() {
  let count = 0;

  function handleClick() {
    count = count + 1;
    console.log(count);
  }

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Clicked {count} times</button>;
}

function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  function handleClick() {
    setCount(count + 1);
  }

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Clicked {count} times</button>;
}

function ProfileForm() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: "Amara", age: 29 });

  function haveBirthdayBroken() {
    user.age = user.age + 1;      // mutates the stored object, no re-render
  }

  function haveBirthdayFixed() {
    setUser({ ...user, age: user.age + 1 }); // new object, triggers re-render
  }

  return <p>{user.name} is {user.age}</p>;
}

