sections.body:
The standard React pattern for a text input is to make it **controlled**: the input's displayed value comes from state, and every keystroke updates that state via `onChange`.

```jsx
function NameField() {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");

  return (
    <input
      value={name}
      onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)}
      placeholder="Enter your name"
    />
  );
}
```

Trace one keystroke: the user presses a key, the browser fires a native input event, React's `onChange` handler runs, you read the typed value off `e.target.value`, call `setName` with it, the component re-renders, and the input's `value` prop is set again, this time to the value you just stored. It feels instantaneous, but it's a round trip on every keystroke, not the DOM tracking what was typed. The payoff: at any instant the input's content *is* your application state, so you can validate it, transform it, mirror it elsewhere in the UI, or disable a submit button based on it by reading that one piece of state, with no step to 'ask the DOM what it currently contains.' The same pattern covers the other form controls with small differences: a checkbox is controlled through `checked` and `e.target.checked` instead of `value`, while `<textarea>` and `<select>` take a `value` prop just like `<input>` (React's `<textarea value={text}>` differs from HTML, where the text goes between the tags). One handler can serve several fields if you give each input a `name` and update with `setForm({ ...form, [e.target.name]: e.target.value })`.

Wire forms up with `<form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>` rather than a click handler on the submit button, since `onSubmit` also fires for other ways of submitting (like pressing Enter inside a text field).

This trips up nearly everyone the first time: the browser's *native* behavior for submitting a form is a page navigation, to the URL in the form's `action` or, by default, the current page (a reload). In a React single-page app that's almost never what you want, since the form's data is already in state. So the first line of a React `onSubmit` handler is almost always `e.preventDefault()`:

```jsx
function handleSubmit(e) {
  e.preventDefault();
  console.log("Submitting:", email, password);
}
```

Forgetting it throws no error: your handler runs, then the browser's default navigation proceeds, which for a same-page form is a full page reload. That wipes out all the React state your app was holding, and to the user the page 'flashed' and reset for no clear reason, which is confusing to debug if you don't know to expect it. (React 19 adds another route: passing a function to `<form action={fn}>` makes React prevent the default navigation for you, but `onSubmit` with `preventDefault()` remains the common pattern for controlled forms.)

examples.code:
function NameField() {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        value={name}
        onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Enter your name"
      />
      <p>You typed: {name}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

function LoginForm() {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState("");
  const [password, setPassword] = useState("");

  function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    console.log("Logging in with", email, password);
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input value={email} onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)} />
      <input type="password" value={password} onChange={(e) => setPassword(e.target.value)} />
      <button type="submit">Log In</button>
    </form>
  );
}

