sections.body:
`&&` short-circuits by returning whichever operand it lands on, which is not necessarily a boolean. That causes a subtle bug when the left-hand side can be `0`:

```jsx
function CartBadge({ itemCount }) {
  return <div>{itemCount && <span className="badge">{itemCount}</span>}</div>;
}
```

When `itemCount` is `0`, `0 && <span>...</span>` evaluates to `0` itself (`&&` returns its left operand when that is falsy, not `false`). React renders numbers as text, just like strings, so a literal "0" appears on the page in exactly the case where you wanted nothing. `false`, `null`, `undefined` (and the empty string) render as nothing, but `0` and `NaN` render as themselves. Make the left-hand side a boolean: `itemCount > 0 && <span>...</span>`, or `Boolean(itemCount) && ...`. Check this whenever the left side of a `&&` could be a number.

To render one component per item in an array, `.map()` each item into a JSX element and put the resulting array in the JSX. React accepts an array of elements as a child just like a single element:

```jsx
function TodoList({ todos }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {todos.map((todo) => (
        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.text}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

That's the standard way to turn an array of data into UI. There is no separate 'list rendering' API; it's the same `.map()` you'd use for any array, returning JSX instead of numbers or strings.

examples.code:
function CartBadge({ itemCount }) {
  // BUGGY: renders a literal "0" when itemCount is 0
  return <div>{itemCount && <span className="badge">{itemCount}</span>}</div>;
}

function FixedCartBadge({ itemCount }) {
  // FIXED: the left side of && is now an actual boolean
  return <div>{itemCount > 0 && <span className="badge">{itemCount}</span>}</div>;
}

// BUGGY: key={index} lets checkbox state 'stick' to a position, not a player
function PlayerList({ players, onSort }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={onSort}>Sort alphabetically</button>
      <ul>
        {players.map((player, index) => (
          <li key={index}>
            {player.name} <input type="checkbox" defaultChecked={player.active} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

// FIXED: key={player.id} follows the actual player, not the list position
function FixedPlayerList({ players, onSort }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={onSort}>Sort alphabetically</button>
      <ul>
        {players.map((player) => (
          <li key={player.id}>
            {player.name} <input type="checkbox" defaultChecked={player.active} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

